{"uncertainty": 0.2, "outcome": "rtl_bug", "artifacts": [{"kind": "root_cause_analysis", "title": "Root cause: uart_tx reloads its hold register on a start pulse while a frame is in flight", "summary": "Failure under investigation: triage reported that the testbench gave 1 of 4 checks failed and reached $finish at 546000 [evidence:prj-d4a591eb4b.triage.e2] [run:run-0002]. VeriTriage classified the log as unknown_failure at 30% confidence, with 'Likely RTL bug' as its top hypothesis [evidence:prj-d4a591eb4b.triage.e1] [run:run-0001]. I treat that hypothesis as one candidate, not as a conclusion.\n\nDefect, located by reading the approved RTL [artifact:prj-d4a591eb4b.design-under-investigation.a1]: in the non-reset branch, the statement 'if (start) hold <= data;' sits outside the state case. So it runs in every state, including START, DATA and STOP. The module's own header states the contract: 'A start pulse while a frame is in flight is ignored.' The IDLE branch already loads hold on start, so the extra statement adds nothing in IDLE. Its only effect is to overwrite hold while busy.\n\nCausality: DATA drives 'tx <= hold[bit_idx + 1]'. Once hold is overwritten mid-frame, every data bit sent after the overwrite comes from the new byte. That gives a wrong bit on the line, and a bit-level check fails. Incorrectness: this breaks the header contract for any start pulse during busy, not only for this test. The fix is to delete the unconditional load. The load inside IDLE then remains the only one.\n\nRanked hypotheses, with the evidence that would refute each:\n(1) rtl_bug, selected. It would be refuted if the repro below passed on this RTL, or failed on the RTL with the unconditional load removed.\n(2) testbench_bug, rejected as the primary cause. The repro's expected values come from the header contract and the 8N1 frame format, not from RTL output. It would be refuted only if the intended specification allowed a reload while busy.\n(3) infrastructure, rejected. The triage run built, reached $finish and reported a counted check failure [run:run-0002]. That is a deterministic functional verdict, not a build, tool or timeout fault. A clean rebuild that changed the result would revive this hypothesis.\n(4) spec_ambiguity, considered and rejected. The behaviour is fixed explicitly by the header sentence quoted above. It would be revived only if a separate specification, which I was not given, contradicted that header.\n\nRepro: the testbench uart_tx_rca_repro.v sends 0x5A cleanly. It then sends 0xA5 and pulses start with 0x3C during data bit 2. It checks every bit at mid-bit, then checks the idle line. On this RTL, data bits 3, 4 and 7 should come out as 0x3C's bits (1, 1, 0) instead of 0xA5's bits (0, 0, 1). On the corrected RTL, all checks should pass."}], "files": [{"path": "uart_tx_rca_repro.v", "kind": "testbench", "title": "Self-checking repro: start pulse during a frame must not alter the transmitted byte", "summary": "Self-checking testbench for uart_tx (CLKS_PER_BIT=4) from [artifact:prj-d4a591eb4b.design-under-investigation.a1]. It sends a clean 0x5A frame, then a 0xA5 frame with a start pulse carrying 0x3C during data bit 2. It samples each start, data and stop bit at mid-bit against values derived from the 8N1 format and the header's 'start pulse while a frame is in flight is ignored' rule. It then checks that the line returns to idle. It counts passed and failed checks and prints one NIRMAAN-SIM verdict with the plusarg nonce before $finish. A watchdog covers hangs. It reproduces the failure class reported in [evidence:prj-d4a591eb4b.triage.e2].", "entry": "uart_tx_rca_repro"}], "tool_runs": ["run:run-0001", "run:run-0002"], "claims": ["The repro is expected to fail on the approved RTL, at data bits 3, 4 and 7 of the 0xA5 frame. I have not run it; the platform's simulator.run gate decides that.", "The repro is expected to pass on RTL with the unconditional 'if (start) hold <= data;' removed. This has not been run.", "The upstream triage testbench was not visible to me. I assume its single failing check reflects the same reload-while-busy defect, but I have not confirmed it."], "escalation": null, "notes": "Traces to [requirement:req-d10c4df532]. Independent review is still required; the design review on record [evidence:prj-d4a591eb4b.design-under-investigation.e1] covers the RTL fixture, not this analysis. Stimulus is driven on negedge and sampling happens on negedge, to avoid races with the posedge-triggered DUT. The only specification available was the RTL header comment. If a separate specification exists that permits a reload while busy, the outcome would change to spec_ambiguity."}
